WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.286 --> 00:00:02.445
- It's something that I
don't usually talk about

00:00:02.445 --> 00:00:06.167
because I'm afraid that I'm
making people uncomfortable.

00:00:06.167 --> 00:00:07.829
It's something that affects me

00:00:07.829 --> 00:00:10.217
in almost every aspect of life.

00:00:10.217 --> 00:00:11.325
(gentle pleasant music)

00:00:11.325 --> 00:00:12.308
(whooshing)

00:00:12.308 --> 00:00:14.891
(hinges creak)

00:00:15.879 --> 00:00:19.235
- There's something about having
dark skin on a woman's body

00:00:19.235 --> 00:00:22.213
that shapes the way she
moves through the world.

00:00:22.213 --> 00:00:25.433
There's something about having
darker skin on a male body

00:00:25.433 --> 00:00:28.353
that shapes the way in which
he moves through the world.

00:00:28.353 --> 00:00:31.077
It has real life stakes.

00:00:31.077 --> 00:00:32.770
- From what I've seen growing up,

00:00:32.770 --> 00:00:35.511
dark skin equates to being poor,

00:00:35.511 --> 00:00:36.344
- dirty,

00:00:36.344 --> 00:00:37.177
- rat shit,

00:00:37.177 --> 00:00:39.760
- they tend to be the bad
guys in Tyler Perry movies,

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:40.875
- straight from the border,

00:00:40.875 --> 00:00:42.763
- that doesn't even make any sense.

00:00:42.763 --> 00:00:45.969
I'm not deemed as beautiful,
therefore I am not beautiful,

00:00:45.969 --> 00:00:48.932
and those two are not
really mutually exclusive.

00:00:48.932 --> 00:00:51.639
- Could it be that something
as early as colonization

00:00:51.639 --> 00:00:55.155
or slavery is primarily responsible

00:00:55.155 --> 00:00:56.970
for our beauty standards today?

00:00:56.970 --> 00:00:58.710
We're talking about who works in the field

00:00:58.710 --> 00:00:59.717
and who works in the house.

00:00:59.717 --> 00:01:03.498
We're also talking about rape,
the rape of slaves to produce

00:01:03.498 --> 00:01:06.608
people who were known as
mulattoes, or mixed race.

00:01:06.608 --> 00:01:08.203
These standards of beauty travel.

00:01:08.203 --> 00:01:10.270
Eurocentric standards of beauty travel.

00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:13.852
- In the Dominican
society and our culture,

00:01:13.852 --> 00:01:17.461
they have a phrase that's like,
(speaks in foreign language)

00:01:17.461 --> 00:01:20.062
advancing the Dominican population

00:01:20.062 --> 00:01:23.535
and making sure we're going
into how they say the better.

00:01:23.535 --> 00:01:25.230
If you're lighter,
you're bettering the race

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:26.466
and you're bettering the island,

00:01:26.466 --> 00:01:29.396
versus if you're just being you.

00:01:29.396 --> 00:01:31.611
- I would get called the blackness.

00:01:31.611 --> 00:01:33.743
One joke that really
stuck with me was like,

00:01:33.743 --> 00:01:36.958
oh, Edgar's so black that
when he leaves a car,

00:01:36.958 --> 00:01:39.232
the check engine light turns on.

00:01:39.232 --> 00:01:41.827
That was a reference to my
skin being as dark as oil.

00:01:41.827 --> 00:01:45.982
I felt ugly being of a darker skin tone,

00:01:45.982 --> 00:01:48.432
so I tried to gel my hair,

00:01:48.432 --> 00:01:50.896
or I started listening
to skater punk music.

00:01:50.896 --> 00:01:53.991
So I tried, culturally, to lighten myself,

00:01:53.991 --> 00:01:55.556
if that makes sense.

00:01:55.556 --> 00:01:57.756
- I went on a trip to Korea with my mom.

00:01:57.756 --> 00:02:01.335
Some woman walked up to
my mom, and she asked,

00:02:01.335 --> 00:02:03.280
"Why is your daughter so dark?"

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:04.896
In Korea, you have to be pale,

00:02:04.896 --> 00:02:07.806
and I almost felt like a foreigner

00:02:07.806 --> 00:02:10.663
and not so much Korean anymore.

00:02:10.663 --> 00:02:12.774
- When we turn off the lights, they say,

00:02:12.774 --> 00:02:14.524
"Oh, Selorm, where did you go?"

00:02:14.524 --> 00:02:18.088
Or, "Oh, you're really pretty
for a dark-skinned girl."

00:02:18.088 --> 00:02:20.861
Are you saying that my
race and just the fact

00:02:20.861 --> 00:02:24.737
that I am black, is that
not attractive in general?

00:02:24.737 --> 00:02:28.872
- I remember one time that I wanted to be

00:02:28.872 --> 00:02:30.622
an angel in a parade.

00:02:31.618 --> 00:02:35.086
At that time, they were like
kind of racist, you know.

00:02:35.086 --> 00:02:38.373
They didn't like black
people to be angels,

00:02:38.373 --> 00:02:40.427
and my mommy got kind of freak out.

00:02:40.427 --> 00:02:43.097
She was going like, "Please don't,

00:02:43.097 --> 00:02:47.194
"you cannot be an angel because
they gonna laugh at you."

00:02:47.194 --> 00:02:49.638
I said, "But mommy, I am an angel."

00:02:49.638 --> 00:02:51.971
And finally, I was an angel.

00:02:53.375 --> 00:02:56.926
And I was the only black
angel walking in the street.

00:02:56.926 --> 00:03:00.762
And my mommy was so proud
about it, and I was proud too.

00:03:00.762 --> 00:03:03.444
- My mom started buying me a
cream called Fair &amp; Lovely.

00:03:03.444 --> 00:03:05.764
I was in third grade when I started using

00:03:05.764 --> 00:03:07.852
(laughs) bleaching creams.

00:03:07.852 --> 00:03:10.316
And the whole general idea was,

00:03:10.316 --> 00:03:12.627
if I'm lighter, I'll have a better life.

00:03:12.627 --> 00:03:15.029
I'll find somebody that's
gonna love me. (laughs)

00:03:15.029 --> 00:03:17.658
There'd still be light and dark patches

00:03:17.658 --> 00:03:22.053
on my body and my face. (sighs)

00:03:22.053 --> 00:03:23.818
It was painful, it would hurt,

00:03:23.818 --> 00:03:27.417
and I started breaking out in bad rashes,

00:03:27.417 --> 00:03:29.244
and no one said anything.

00:03:29.244 --> 00:03:30.847
I had to stop it myself.

00:03:30.847 --> 00:03:32.422
- How do we fix this?

00:03:32.422 --> 00:03:34.586
We think critically about colorism.

00:03:34.586 --> 00:03:36.704
We think about where it began,

00:03:36.704 --> 00:03:38.905
and where it begins and ends with us.

00:03:38.905 --> 00:03:43.072
- I think change starts with
providing more diversity.

00:03:44.389 --> 00:03:45.596
You know, providing more examples

00:03:45.596 --> 00:03:47.547
of people that look like you.

00:03:47.547 --> 00:03:49.322
- If you look at telenovelas,
they usually cast

00:03:49.322 --> 00:03:51.139
the dark-skinned person as the villain,

00:03:51.139 --> 00:03:53.218
and if you never met a Latino before,

00:03:53.218 --> 00:03:55.039
you're gonna somewhat think that.

00:03:55.039 --> 00:03:58.253
- So in Japanese media,
I honestly don't think

00:03:58.253 --> 00:04:02.330
I've ever seen a darker-skinned female.

00:04:02.330 --> 00:04:05.071
I think it would be nice to see a change.

00:04:05.071 --> 00:04:06.902
- It needs to start
within our own culture,

00:04:06.902 --> 00:04:08.835
where we can't have Will Smith

00:04:08.835 --> 00:04:10.934
filling in every slot for black people.

00:04:10.934 --> 00:04:13.466
Giving dark-skinned people roles

00:04:13.466 --> 00:04:16.183
that are not only supporting
roles but leading roles.

00:04:16.183 --> 00:04:20.261
- I mean a legit, badass,
007 Latino James Bond,

00:04:20.261 --> 00:04:22.176
that'd be pretty dope, I'm just sayin'.

00:04:22.176 --> 00:04:25.077
- If I could give a
message to someone like me,

00:04:25.077 --> 00:04:28.789
- who's kind of looking at
himself and feeling insecure,

00:04:28.789 --> 00:04:30.621
- just know, baby, you beautiful.

00:04:30.621 --> 00:04:32.294
- Believe in yourself.

00:04:32.294 --> 00:04:33.836
- Just be you.

00:04:33.836 --> 00:04:35.935
- You are perfect just the way you are.

00:04:35.935 --> 00:04:38.597
That's something that
no one has ever told me.

00:04:38.597 --> 00:04:41.015
- And your skin color is a gift.

00:04:41.015 --> 00:04:43.502
It's never been a blemish or a problem.

00:04:43.502 --> 00:04:46.137
- When you grow up and
you look in the mirror,

00:04:46.137 --> 00:04:49.317
and you're proud of who you
are, it will make sense,

00:04:49.317 --> 00:04:52.846
and all the other voices will go away.

00:04:52.846 --> 00:04:55.020
And all that's gonna be left is you.

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:58.353
(gentle pleasant music)

00:05:03.638 --> 00:05:04.744
(whooshing)

00:05:04.744 --> 00:05:07.327
(hinges creak)

